-- Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two, three combat vehicles (printed 231-235): Mammoth APC, Road Buster, Sentinel Patrol Hovercar
-- 3 vehicle rows, with 39 locations and 7 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~327-northern-gun-2-combat-vehicles-b.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-at46-mammoth', 'NG-AT46 Mammoth Military APC and Cargo Transport', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Five: a driver in the nose cockpit, plus a co-pilot, a communications officer, a gunner and a passenger seated behind in the crew compartment, which has a second set of driving controls worked through cameras and instruments.', 'The lower troop deck seats 12-14 and the upper observation deck 6-8 (half that in power armor). The open-air bay takes 18-24 infantry in EBA or 8-10 troops in power armor, and 8-14 more can ride on the rails and mounts along the bed and roof.', '90 mph (144 km) maximum on paved and dirt roads, gravel, rocky desert, grassland, broken roads and rough ground; half that or less on sandy desert and in water or snow 1-5 ft (0.3-1.5 m) deep. Woodland and thick brush are likely impassable without a well-used path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km). Speed drops 30% with a full or near-full load.', NULL, 'Drives through water up to 10 ft (3 m) deep; any deeper and it stalls and sinks.', 'Height 23 ft (7 m); width 16 ft (4.9 m); length 38 ft (11.6 m)', '12 tons empty', 445, 1, NULL, 23000000, '26 million credits nuclear, 23 million Solid Oxide. Fair to poor availability.', 'Model NG-AT46 (Class: All-Terrain Armored Personnel Carrier and Cargo Transport). One of Northern Gun''s largest and newest combat vehicles, out since spring 109 P.A. from a design team led by Denny Ham, Chad Lasecki, Shel Carltonaw and K.S. Shems. Heavily armoured yet quick for its bulk, it carries armoured and power-armoured troops or heavy freight over most ground and mounts enough guns to guard them or support a battle. Mercenary companies, independent nations and city-states have taken to it eagerly; the Coalition States admire the design but dislike seeing it in rivals'' hands, and Free Quebec feels the same. Layout: a nose cockpit; a reinforced crew compartment for four with stowage for weapons and 5-6 packs, rifles and canteens; then two sealed environmental troop decks; and an open bay at the back. The lower deck has benches for 12-14. The upper observation deck, above it and behind the cockpit, seats 6-8 (half as many in power armor) and has 30 M.D.C. plexiglass windows with 50 M.D.C. sliding shields for combat. Both decks have a 7 ft (2.1 m) ceiling, so tall soldiers and power armor must stoop. Troops leave by the two side hatches, and the upper deck also by the roof hatch, one at a time. Cargo: the open bay, 16 ft (4.9 m) long and 7 ft (2.1 m) wide with 6 ft (1.8 m) walls, holds 32 tons: freight, 1-4 small vehicles, one small or medium robot standing or crouched, 18-24 EBA-clad infantry or 8-10 troops in power armor, more if they sit on the side containers or ride outside. Sealed containers along its walls (15 M.D.C. per 4 ft/1.2 m section and per hatch) protect food, medicine, water, ammunition, guns and electronics. More cargo, or 8-14 more troops, can be strapped to rails and mounts on the bed sides and roof. Front and rear winches tow trailers, free stuck vehicles and drag wreckage; towing capacity is 40 tons at half speed. Penalties: none on pavement, grass, dirt roads, gravel, stony desert and broken roads; -5% piloting on sand and in mud up to 2 ft (0.6 m); -10% in water or snow 6-10 ft (1.8-3 m) deep and on ice. Forest, dense growth and narrow mountain paths are likely impassable. Range: unlimited. Power: Solid Oxide or nuclear (10 year life). Sensors: all the standard robot features. With the Main Body gone it shuts down and is useless.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.231-233'),
  ('ng-at26-road-buster', 'NG-AT26 Road Buster Armored Combat Truck', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Up to three in the cab, one beside the driver and two behind (a third can squeeze in behind, uncomfortably); six troops sit or kneel in the bed, eight packed in.', '90 mph (144 km) maximum on paved and dirt roads, gravel, rocky desert, grassland, broken roads and rough ground; half that or less on sandy desert and in water or snow 1-5 ft (0.3-1.5 m) deep. Woodland and thick brush are likely impassable without a well-used path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km).', NULL, 'Drives through water up to 5 ft (1.5 m) deep.', 'Height 10 ft (3 m); width 11 ft (3.4 m); length 24 ft (7.3 m)', '3.3 tons empty', 245, 1, NULL, 586000, '1.1 million credits nuclear, 835,000 Solid Oxide, 586,000 electric battery or liquid fuel. A hovercycle weapon option costs extra.', 'Model NG-AT26 (Class: All-Terrain Armored Truck and Reconnaissance Vehicle). A versatile Northern Gun truck for freight, carrying the wounded, taking a small squad on patrol, scouting and border work, with or without 2-6 troops riding in the bed. Built for front-line and scouting duty, it can take heavy fire and keep moving. The text gives it six big armoured combat wheels that cope with most ground, sand and snow included, and grip well under heavy loads; its M.D.C. table lists four back and four front. A winch and cable at each end can work together to rope-bridge the truck over a river or chasm, and they also free stuck vehicles, pull stumps, drag wreckage and tow trailers or cargo. It has no built-in weapons: a passenger can stand in the roof hatch to shoot a hand-held gun from up high. For extra cost one hovercycle weapon option can be fitted, usually on the roof, and only Solid Oxide or nuclear models can power it; some owners build a manned gun station or mini-missile launcher into the bed, losing all or 66% of its cargo space. Penalties: none on pavement, grass, dirt roads, gravel, stony desert and broken roads; -5% piloting on sand and in mud up to 1 ft (0.3 m); -10% in water or snow 1-5 ft (0.3-1.5 m) deep and on ice. Forest and dense growth are likely impassable; the page also says it can cross dunes and go underwater, though its water limit is 5 ft (1.5 m). Range: 400 miles (640 km) on gasoline, 1,500 miles (2,400 km) on one charge of the multi-battery electric system, unlimited on Solid Oxide or nuclear (10 year life); every power system is offered. Cargo: a bed 8 ft (2.4 m) long and 5 ft (1.5 m) wide with 2 ft (0.6 m) walls, room in the cab for 5-6 backpacks and several weapons, and more gear strapped to the running boards, bed sides and roof. Features: a bare-bones, non-environmental vehicle with a basic short-range radio, a cigarette lighter, a disk player and the two winches. With the Main Body gone it shuts down and is useless.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.233-234'),
  ('ng-hc46-sentinel', 'NG-HC46 Sentinel Patrol Hovercar', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Up to three: one in front and two in the back seat.', 'Hover only: from a stationary hover to 140 mph (224 km) maximum, riding 1-10 ft (0.3-3 m) above the ground.', 'Cannot fly; hovers no more than 10 ft (3 m) up.', 'None.', 'Height 6.7 ft (2 m); width 8 ft (2.4 m); length 20 ft (6.1 m)', '2.5 tons', 200, 1, NULL, 1700000, '2.1 million credits nuclear (about 12 year life), 1.7 million Solid Oxide.', 'Model NG-HC46 (Class: Hover Capable Armored Patrol Car). An armoured sentry car for officers, border and perimeter patrols and escort duty, from a design team headed by Chris Haacke, M.T. Zarchron and Stewart Easton, who set out to build one fast, well-protected hover patrol car for militias, police, mercenaries, army officers and wealthy people worried for their safety. All of those buy it, and so do Body Fixers, scientists and merchants working out of villages and towns in dangerous country, and anyone wanting a combat car with some flair: Crazies, Juicers, Gunfighters, Headhunters, Sheriffs, some Black Market agents, and some highwaymen, raiders, Cyber-Snatchers and bandits who like its mix of armour, guns and speed. It is more and more common in wilderness communities, and as a hovercar it copes with most ground except woodland and sandy desert. Cargo: behind the back seat there is room for six backpacks, 4 energy rifles, some water containers and a few odds and ends. Power: nuclear (about 12 years) or Solid Oxide. Sensors: as a robot vehicle. Each undercarriage hover jet lost cuts speed 10% and costs -5% driving. With the Main Body gone the car is destroyed beyond repair.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.234-235');

